1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,440
Fine dust reaching your gut — if that's news to you, save this now.

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:08,640
Fine dust isn't just a breathing problem.

3
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:16,400
Three things are behind it: swallowed particles, a weakened gut lining, and an imbalance in gut bacteria.

4
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:25,520
First, the mucus that traps particles in your nose and airways gets swept to the back of your throat and swallowed, several times a day.

5
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:33,040
That means fine dust doesn't just stay in your lungs — it travels straight into your stomach and intestines.

6
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:40,480
Second, research shows swallowed particles can loosen the tight junctions between the cells lining your gut.

7
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:48,560
Once that barrier weakens, bacteria and waste products can leak through, a pattern often called "leaky gut."

8
00:00:48,880 --> 00:01:01,040
Finally, fine dust exposure has been linked to shifts in gut bacteria: fewer of the beneficial strains that produce short-chain fatty acids, and more of the strains tied to inflammation.

9
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:07,760
Some studies have even connected heavy pollution exposure to a higher risk of inflammatory bowel disease.

10
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:17,360
On high pollution days, wear a mask to cut down what you swallow, and load up on fiber-rich vegetables to support your gut lining.

11
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:21,520
Probiotics can also help rebuild that bacterial balance.
